Stochastic propagation models in packet-level 5G simulators cannot reproduce site-specific multipath, yet ray tracing is computationally prohibitive at simulation rates.
    We present \texttt{sionnart}, an ns-3 extension module that embeds the Sionna RT ray tracing engine directly into the ns-3 process via pybind11, exposing deterministic channel information through standard ns-3 propagation interfaces with no inter-process overhead.
    Practical performance for user equipment(UE) under mobility is achieved through two mechanisms: a displacement-based coherence cache that reuses channel parameters within a geometry-derived validity radius, and adaptive virtual receiver generation that pre-computes predicted near-future UE positions in the same batched Sionna RT call.
    In first part of experiment with 32 UEs, \texttt{sionnart} is $232.3\times$ faster than the socket-coupled \texttt{ns3sionna} baseline (stationary) and $43.8\times$ faster under high mobility, with GPU memory below 500~MiB.
    In different propagation environment scenarios with 20 UEs and $2\times2$ to $8\times8$ gNB arrays across free-space, urban macro, and urban micro environments, deterministic multipath consistently shifts CQI, MCS selection, throughput, packet loss, and gNB energy relative to stochastic models, with divergence growing in geometry-sensitive deployments.
    These results establish that embedded ray tracing enables repeatable, geometry-aware system-level evaluation of massive MIMO energy and performance trade-offs at full protocol-stack fidelity.
